<h2>Emails</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>A subject that reads like a person</strong>: “Re: your enquiry, Sam” beats “Brightside Ltd – Services Information”.</li>
  <li><strong>Open with them</strong>, not you. Mention what they asked about in the first line.</li>
  <li><strong>Short</strong>: under 120 words. If it scrolls on a phone, cut it.</li>
  <li><strong>One question, one next step.</strong> Make it easy to answer: a yes or no, or a choice of two times.</li>
  <li><strong>Sign with your name</strong>, not just the business.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Text messages</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Say who you are in the first few words: they don’t have your number saved.</li>
  <li>Only text people who gave you their mobile number, and keep to business hours.</li>
  <li>One or two sentences. No links on a first text; they look like spam.</li>
  <li>If they ask you to stop, stop, and note it on the lead.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Before you send</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Read it out loud. If you wouldn’t say it, don’t write it.</li>
  <li>Check every “…” in a template or AI draft is filled in.</li>
  <li>Never promise a price, date or result you haven’t checked.</li>
</ul>
